  "## The claim that was false

CLAUDE.md records that shallow clones were retired 2026-07-21 (ADR-2607211600,
reversing the earlier shallow-by-default) and that **everything was
unshallowed**. That is stated as completed history, and work today relied on it.

**It is not true.** A scan of every checkout — 4,407 `.git` directories plus 7
`.git`-file pointers, keyed on the `shallow` marker file, which is definitive
for both layouts — found **11 shallow child repos, plus the superproject root
itself**, plus two orphan shallow gitdirs with no checkout.

## Why it mattered today

`orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba` was one of them: 24 grafts, 4 false root commits, an
apparent history beginning 2026-07-14, and only 234 commits reachable.

**It is the one repo where ancestry questions keep being asked**, because it
holds the deleted `kotoba-server` source (ADR-2608124000). An investigation
concluded from that clone that the deployed build commit was reachable only from
a stale side branch; the GitHub API said it is on `main`'s history, 643 commits
back. **The local answer was wrong and looked authoritative.**

Unshallowed, it now reproduces the API exactly and locally:

- 234 -> **1,487 commits**, 4 false roots -> **1 true root**
- `merge-base --is-ancestor <build> origin/main` -> **yes**
- distance to `origin/main`: **643** — the ADR's server-side figure
- deletion commit: ancestor, **584** back — also matching
- `branch -r --contains <build>`: **57 branches including `origin/main`**, not a
  stale side branch
- path-limited `git log -- crates/kotoba-server` works again; the crate is there
  at the build commit, 52 files

**The question ADR-2608124000 had to route through the API is now answerable on
this machine.**

## CORRECTION: nothing re-shallowed it yesterday. That was my inference, and it was wrong.

The section below reads the `18:50` mtime as evidence of a recent shallowing.
**It is a `git gc` artifact.** `git repack -d` / `gc` calls `prune_shallow()`,
which rewrites `.git/shallow` through a lock-and-rename — **bumping the mtime
with byte-identical content.**

Verified experimentally with the same git binary: in a scratch shallow clone,
`git gc --prune=now` bumped the mtime and left the graft unchanged; on a
non-shallow control, gc created no shallow file at all. **gc can rewrite a
shallow file but never creates one.**

The root's `.git` carries gc's whole fingerprint from that evening in the exact
order the experiment reproduced — mass reflog rewrite 18:47:50, pack index and
`.rev` 18:49, `info/refs` 18:49, `shallow` 18:50.

**When it actually happened.** The 10 graft SHAs all date to 2026-07-18 to
07-22, and simulating that frontier at the recorded HEAD yields 11,994 reachable
against the 12,077 measured — the right frontier. Two properties identify the
operation: each graft's parents are dated within minutes of it, several *later*,
so **no date cutoff separates them** (not `--shallow-since`); and all 10 sit at a
uniform min-distance from a single main-line tip, **which is the signature of
`--depth N` on one branch.**

Bracketed by two independent records: an ADR measured the root non-shallow with
full history on 2026-08-01, and a byte copy of the root's `.git` made by another
session at 2026-08-11 14:57 holds the **identical 10 SHAs** — proving the root
was already shallow **four hours before** the mtime I read as the event.

**So: a `--depth N` fetch somewhere between 2026-08-01 and 2026-08-11.** It left
no trace in any reachable log and is not attributable to committed tooling —
consistent with a one-off ad-hoc command, which is **a different risk profile
from a scheduled job** and means no fix is owed to any script.

Every other shallow repo here has its shallow-file mtime at its frontier date.
**The root is the only one where they diverge, because it is the only one busy
enough to have been gc'd since.** That divergence is exactly what misled me.

## The structural hazard this exposed, which is the real finding

**Linked worktrees share `$GIT_COMMON_DIR/shallow` with the repository they were
made from.** Confirmed: a worktree under `/private/tmp` reports the
superproject's own `.git` as its common dir.

So **a `--depth` fetch inside any `throwaway` worktree shallows the superproject
itself**, and the root currently has four linked worktrees, three of them under
`/private/tmp`.

This workspace *instructs* agents to work in worktrees. That guidance is right
for isolation of the working tree — **but the object store, and the shallow file,
are not isolated at all.** A worktree is not a sandbox for anything that writes
to `.git`. That is very likely how the unattributed fetch reached the root, and
it is the part worth carrying into practice rather than the date.

## Ruled out, each checked against the ROOT specifically

west (no `clone-depth`, no `.west` depth, root is not a west project); git config
(no `gc.*`, `fetch.*`, depth, maintenance or prune settings anywhere, no
`git maintenance` launchd job); the four surviving `--depth 1` callers in
`scripts/` (all clone GitHub URLs into throwaway temp workspaces, none opens the
root's `.git`); 43 launchd plists, of which the only two doing shallow git work
target a `mkdtemp` directory and a private repo; the previously known offender,
which ADR-2608019100 caught fetching `--depth 1` into the root every six hours
and which **no longer exists**; and every session transcript and shell history in
the window — all `--depth` hits clone other repos elsewhere.

**No `unrelated histories` symptom appears anywhere in the window**, which is
consistent with a ~12,000-commit-deep frontier: invisible to everyday merge-base
work. **That is why nobody noticed.**

## The 5.5 GB repo should NOT be unshallowed. It is a stale duplicate.

`orgs/gftdcojp/ai-gftd-apps-gftdcojp` is the **pre-rename path**.
`manifest/repos.edn` carries the rename and GitHub confirms the redirect — both
names resolve to one repository.

**The registered path `orgs/gftdcojp/apps-gftdcojp` already has full history**:
not shallow, 27,976 commits, zero WIP, same HEAD. So **the ancestry question is
already answerable on this machine**, at the other path.

The old path is worse than recorded: **HEAD is itself a graft**, so
`rev-list --count HEAD` returns **1**. Depth 1 from the pin — it can answer no
ancestry question at all. And **nothing reads it**: not in `west.yml`, not in
`fleet-db.edn`, not in `fleet-ci.edn`, untracked in the superproject. The one
tool doing local `merge-base --is-ancestor` iterates west projects only, so it
never visits this path.

**So the benefit of unshallowing is zero and the cost is 5.4 GiB.** The real
opportunity is the reverse: **retiring the checkout frees ~11 GB.** Of its 39
local branches, 10 tips are absent from the new checkout and **all 10 were
verified present on GitHub**. The genuine blocker is 11 uncommitted files inside
a **stale-locked** worktree — its lock names a process that is not running, last
commit 2026-07-03 — and those are by definition not on GitHub. That retirement
belongs in the `git-cleanup-conflict` `:retirement` flow, and involves another
session's worktree.

One thing that only *looks* like WIP: the 3 modified files at the old path are
**byte-identical to the new path's `main`**, landed by a cleanup commit with the
identical diffstat. They appear uncommitted solely because this checkout's HEAD
is pinned at a graft predating that commit.

**The stale temp pack is gone.** 580,004,872 bytes, verified orphaned five ways
before removal — git itself called it garbage, it had **no `.idx`** so the pack
loader could not reach it by construction, no process held it open, it was 24
days old, and **its retry succeeded**: the interrupted fetch declared 113,157
objects and a complete pack landed 32 minutes later with 196,797. No `gc`,
`prune` or `repack` was run.

## The worktree hazard is live in committed tooling

One of the three violations below was **not** a scratch clone. It fetches
`--depth 1` into a path created by `git worktree add`, and **linked worktrees
share the shallow file with their parent repository** — so that scheduled job
writes grafts into the parent's object store. **This is the exact mechanism this
ADR identifies as how the superproject likely got re-shallowed, running weekly
on a timer.**

## Two live violations, and an audit wired to nothing

**All three are fixed** — the audit went 6 violations to 3, and the remaining 3
are unrelated pre-existing items. Both launchd jobs are still loaded and
scheduled; no plist was touched.

**The shallow bought 174 KB.** The `--depth 50` clone was into a *persistent*
path refreshed daily, not scratch. Unshallowed: 86 to 136 commits, 18 grafts to
0, **7.9 seconds**, and the pack grew from 47.04 to 47.21 MiB. No
B2/DataLad argument was needed; full history cost 0.17 MiB.

**Two corrections to the brief, both worth more than the fix.**

The playtest job's target repo is **not missing — it changed org**, and is
checked out, not shallow, 855 commits. The script's hardcoded path is stale on
both counts. And it does not fail: **`spawnSync` with a nonexistent `cwd`
returns `status: nil`, the script ignores return values, so every command
silently no-ops** and it prints one benign line and exits 0. The log's last four
entries are exactly that line with nothing between them — **four consecutive
weekly no-ops that read as healthy in `launchctl` and whose log line reads like a
real outcome.** That is worse than an erroring job.

**The other job has failed every run since it was created** — 21 log lines for 21
expected daily runs, 20 HTTP 400 and one 502, never once succeeding. The cause is
visible: it hardcodes a concrete model id against a legacy hostname, violating
ADR-2607173100, **and the `murakumo-main` alias currently resolves that same
model to a different endpoint.** Left alone deliberately: changing the LLM call
on a job that auto-merges to `main` deserves an explicit decision.

Verification limits stated rather than implied: the shinshi edit was exercised by
running the real edited function, and the repo **stayed non-shallow** afterwards
— but the script was deliberately not run end to end, because it commits, pushes
and **auto-merges to `main` of a live repo**. The playtest depth-flag removal is
**unexercised**, because its target directory does not exist so the fetch never
executes.

**`~/.gftd` is not a git repository, and 26 launchd plists point into it.** These
are scheduled, they violated a repo-wide ADR for weeks, and nothing reviews them.
They should be version-controlled and the audit should become a fleet-ci gate
rather than a script someone remembers to run — but the plists carry absolute
paths, so **relocating the files breaks 26 jobs unless the plists move in the
same step.**

Also worth removing: the byte copy of the root's `.git` at `/private/tmp` still
holds the shallow frontier. **Fetching from it would re-import the grafts.**

## Will it recur? No scheduled job will redo it

The root is clean — `is-shallow: false`, no shallow file, 25,092 commits — and
with the file gone gc has nothing to rewrite, so the unshallow is stable.

What would catch the next one: git records fetch arguments in reflog messages,
**but a depth fetch that does not move a ref writes no entry**, which is why this
one is invisible. A `reference-transaction` hook or a watcher on `.git/shallow`
would name the responsible process.

## (original, now corrected) The superproject was re-shallowed YESTERDAY

`.git/shallow` in the superproject root carried **10 grafts** and its mtime is
**2026-08-11 18:50** — three weeks *after* the retirement. **That is not 2026-07-21
residue; something re-shallowed it recently.** The root is not a west project,
so west did not do it, and what did was not determined.

It is now unshallowed: **12,077 -> 25,062 commits, 10 grafts -> 6 true root
commits.** (A concurrent session moved `origin/main` mid-fetch, producing a ref
lock warning; the unshallow itself completed.)

This one matters beyond ancestry hygiene: **the superproject is where pin
verification, merge-base and fast-forward checks are performed.** Those are the
operations ADR-2607211600 retired shallow to protect.

## Re-shallowing risk from west: measured clear

`manifest/west.yml` has **zero** occurrences of `clone-depth` or `depth`,
`.west/config` has no depth setting, and no `remote.*.depth` exists in git
config. `gen-west-manifest.cljs` documents that the `clone-depth` emission
machinery was **removed 2026-07-25 precisely because it silently reintroduced
`clone-depth: 1`**. The surviving `--depth 1` calls all clone into throwaway
temporary workspaces, never into `orgs/`.

**So these unshallows will stick.** What re-shallowed the root on 2026-08-11 is
therefore *not* west, and remains unexplained.

## Remediation: 9 of the 10 remaining are done

Unshallowed, ~740 MB total, 51 s for the largest: `kotodama`, `kotoba-code`,
`hil`, `tamaki`, `kotoba-topology`, `kototama`, `ai-gftd-yukkuri`,
`kotoba-v2025`, and `etzhayyim/root` (20 grafts, 10,122 -> 10,183 commits, 54 ->
47 roots).

**Safety confirmed rather than assumed.** Every repo's fetch refspec was checked
first: all map **only** into `refs/remotes/origin/*`, none writes `refs/heads/*`.
Combined with detached HEAD everywhere (normal at a west pin), **no local branch
and no HEAD could move.** `git status --porcelain` was hashed before and after
each and is byte-identical — including `etzhayyim/root`'s pre-existing WIP, which
survived. `-c gc.auto=0` was passed on every fetch, since `fetch` can trigger
auto-gc.

**`gftdcojp/ai-gftd-apps-gftdcojp` (50 grafts, 5.5 GB) was skipped**, for four
reasons rather than size alone: it is ~90% of the remaining liability; it has a
**locked** agent worktree plus a second on a live branch, i.e. concurrent
sessions; it carries a **580 MB stale `tmp_pack_` from 19 July**, which is direct
evidence that a large fetch here has already died mid-flight; and it has
uncommitted WIP. A failed 5.5 GB fetch leaves more of the same.

Two honest residuals, both real rather than graft artifacts. `kototama` keeps
**one graft** whose commit is reachable from no ref — the orphaned detached HEAD
of another session's linked worktree, left rather than disturbed; its own history
is now genuinely full. And `etzhayyim/root` shows 47 roots with **zero** grafts,
which is expected for a monorepo that absorbed many former submodule histories.

Two orphan shallow gitdirs remain with no checkout (submodule-era leftovers,
both carrying the now-banned `-clj` suffix). **No checkout means no ancestry
answer can come from them**, so unshallowing them buys nothing.

## The original list of 11

Remediating all of them would pull roughly **7 GB**, dominated by one 5.5 GB
repo. They are recorded here rather than fixed, so the decision is visible:

`gftdcojp/ai-gftd-apps-gftdcojp` (50 grafts, 5.5 GB) · `etzhayyim/root` (20,
596 MB) · `kotoba-lang/kotoba-v2025` · `gftdcojp/ai-gftd-yukkuri` ·
`kotoba-lang/kototama` · `kotoba-lang/kotoba-topology` · `etzhayyim/tamaki` ·
`kotoba-lang/hil` · `kotodama` · `kotoba-code`.

## `git fetch --dry-run --unshallow` is not a preview

Reported honestly by the agent that hit it: **`--dry-run` skips ref updates but
still performs the fetch.** Running it downloaded the full history and removed
the `shallow` file — the intended operation happened through a command expected
to be inert.

The outcome was correct and nothing was at risk, but the lesson generalises:
**`--dry-run` on `fetch` is not read-only.** That checkout had 33 lines of
uncommitted WIP and a detached HEAD, so it was worth knowing before rather than
after.

## The gitdir defect: diagnosed, deliberately left

`orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba`'s `.git` is a file pointing at a leftover **submodule**
gitdir under the repo's **old org path**. **It is functionally correct**, which
the first check got wrong: `core.worktree` is not in `config` but in
`config.worktree`, enabled by `extensions.worktreeConfig`, and points forward to
the current checkout. Both links are relative and at equal depth. The only
functional symptom is `git worktree list` reporting the gitdir path.

A move would be mechanically clean — no alternates, no linked worktrees, no
gitlink, no `.gitmodules`, and the old path does not exist. **It was still not
done**, because the benefit is cosmetic while the risk lands on 9 untracked,
nowhere-backed-up source files in a repo with a detached HEAD and an apparently
active session, and the operation is **non-atomic across two locations** — between
the move and the pointer rewrite the checkout is broken.

Precondition if it is ever wanted: land or archive the WIP, confirm no live
session, then move and rewrite in one scripted step."}]
